      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitetop%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community sits at 5,520 feet, making it the highest point in Virginia that is still reachable by vehicle.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.whitetopva.org/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Whitetop marks the southern terminus of the 34‑mile Virginia Creeper Trail, a popular path for biking, hiking, and horseback riding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        28,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitetop%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s annual Ramp Festival celebrates spring with ramps and bluegrass music, drawing visitors from the surrounding region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.whitetopva.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Whitetop lies just four miles northeast of the North Carolina–Tennessee–Virginia corner, a unique tri‑state junction nearby.",
